course: everyday
lesson: 326
title: Rainbows, beach earthquakes, lambs, blackberries, cacti, fire ants and bee stings
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A rainbow forms when sunlight goes into raindrops, bends and comes back out split into colours, so to watch a rainbow you stand with the sun behind you and look towards the rain.
explain: A rainbow is light, not a thing in one place, so you can never walk to the end of a rainbow: it moves as you move.
explain: An earthquake at the beach is special: if the shaking is long or strong, a tsunami may follow within minutes, so you go to high ground or inland at once. «Long or strong, get gone.»
explain: Example: a boy on a beach near Napier in New Zealand feels the ground shake for almost a minute. He does not wait for a siren; he runs with his family up the hill behind the town.
explain: Nature is lovely to look at but not always to touch: lambs in spring fields, blackberries on a hedge, cacti in the desert and fire ant nests all need care.
explain: Speak quietly in nature and leave loud music at home, because noise frightens animals and disturbs other visitors.
fact: To see a rainbow, stand with the sun behind you and look towards the rain.
fact: A rainbow forms when sunlight bends inside raindrops and splits into colours; you cannot reach the end of a rainbow.
fact: If an earthquake at the beach or on the coast is long or strong, go to high ground or inland at once, because a tsunami may come within minutes.
fact: In spring, look at the lambs in the fields but keep away and keep dogs on a lead, because ewes panic and dogs can chase and hurt sheep.
fact: Pick blackberries only when a grown-up has identified them, above dog height and away from roads, and wash them first, because some berries look alike and some are poisonous.
fact: Look at cacti but do not touch them: cactus spines go into skin and are hard to get out.
fact: Keep away from ant hills and never sit on a fire ant nest; fire ants in parts of the USA and Australia sting many times.
fact: Making a lot of noise in nature frightens animals and disturbs other visitors, so speak quietly.
fact: A honeybee usually dies after stinging a person, because its barbed sting gets stuck in the skin.
fact: It is a myth that every bee dies when it stings: wasps and bumblebees can sting again and do not die.
fact: After a bee sting, scrape the sting out sideways with a fingernail, cool the spot and tell an adult; get help at once if it swells a lot or breathing is hard.
exercise: Where should the sun be when you are watching a rainbow? | Behind you, while you look towards the rain | In front of you, shining straight into your eyes | Directly above your head at noon | Hidden behind a thick cloud
exercise: Why can you never reach the end of a rainbow? | It is light bent in raindrops and moves as you move | It is always over the sea, too far away | It fades away before anyone can get there | It is guarded by strong winds that blow you back near the ground
exercise: A girl is on a beach in California when an earthquake shakes hard for a long time. What should she do? | Go to high ground or inland at once | Wait on the sand for an official warning | Go to the water's edge to watch the sea | Lie down flat on the beach until evening
exercise: Why is a long or strong earthquake at the beach so dangerous? | A tsunami may follow within minutes | The sand turns into deep quicksand | The sea becomes too cold to swim in | Seagulls attack people after the shaking
exercise: Lambs are in the fields in spring and you are walking your dog. What do you do? | Look from a distance and keep the dog on a lead | Let the dog off so it can play with them | Climb in over the gate and pick up a lamb to cuddle it | Call the lambs over to the fence with food
exercise: Why should you keep away from lambs in a field? | The mother sheep may panic and dogs can hurt sheep | Lambs bite very hard and can carry dangerous rabies to people | Lambs are shy and will never grow if touched | Farmers fine anyone who looks at their sheep
exercise: You find blackberries on a hedge by a path. Which berries are safe to pick? | Ones a grown-up has identified, high up, away from roads | Any dark berries low down near the path, as long as they look ripe and juicy | Any berry that birds have already pecked | The shiniest berries closest to the road
exercise: Why must a grown-up identify berries before you eat them? | Many berries look alike and some are poisonous | Berries always taste sour and bitter until an adult has checked them | Wild berries are only for birds by law | Washing berries makes any berry safe to eat
exercise: A boy in Arizona sees a fluffy-looking cactus in a desert garden. What should he do? | Look at it but not touch it | Stroke it gently, as fluffy ones are soft | Break off a piece to take home | Touch it only with one finger
exercise: Why should you not touch a cactus? | Its spines go into skin and are hard to get out | Its juice is a strong poison that burns the skin for days | Cacti bite when they feel a hand nearby | Touching kills the cactus within a day
exercise: At a picnic in Texas you see a big mound of soil full of ants. What do you do? | Keep away and sit somewhere else | Sit on it, as the soil is soft | Poke it with a stick to see the ants | Pour your drink on it to chase them off
exercise: What makes fire ants in parts of the USA and Australia dangerous? | They sting many times | They spit fire at people | They bite once and then die | They carry the flu in their legs
exercise: Your cousin wants to play loud music on a forest walk. What is best? | Leave the music at home and speak quietly | Play it loud so the animals can enjoy it | Play it only near birds' nests | Shout to each other instead of playing music, so you don't get lost
exercise: Why should you be quiet in nature? | Noise frightens animals and disturbs other visitors | Noise makes the trees stop growing and drop their leaves early | Loud sounds cause rain to fall | Animals are deaf, so it does not matter
exercise: Which is true about the myth that a bee dies every time it stings? | Honeybees usually die after stinging, but wasps and bumblebees do not | Every bee and wasp dies after one sting | No bee ever dies from stinging someone | Only bumblebees die after stinging
exercise: Why does a honeybee usually die after stinging a person? | Its barbed sting gets stuck in the skin | It uses up all its poison in one go and then starves | The person's blood poisons the bee | It is too frightened to fly again
test: You see the sun low in the west after a shower. Where should you look to find a rainbow? | Towards the east, where the rain is | Straight towards the sun in the west | Down at the ground near your feet | Anywhere, it does not matter
test: A friend says there is treasure at the end of the rainbow and you will walk there. What is true? | The rainbow moves as you move, so there is no end to reach | The end can be reached if you walk fast enough before it fades | The end is always in the nearest field | The end appears only when the rain stops
test: On holiday in Oregon, a family on the beach feel the ground shake for a long time. There is no siren yet. What is right? | Go to high ground straight away without waiting | Wait on the beach for the siren before moving anywhere | Watch the sea to see if it goes out | Go into a beach hut and shut the door
test: A girl in Wales lets her puppy off the lead in a field of ewes and lambs. Why is this a bad idea? | The dog may chase and hurt the sheep and they panic | The lambs might all chase and bite the puppy, and it would get hurt | The grass in sheep fields poisons dogs | The farmer will want to keep the puppy
test: Which blackberries would be the best choice to pick? | Ones high on a hedge in a park, checked by Dad and washed | Ones at the bottom of a hedge by a busy road | Ones that look a bit like blackberries but are smaller | Ones on any bush, eaten straight away
test: Which is true about cacti? | Their spines get stuck in skin and are hard to remove | They are perfectly safe if you touch them very quickly | Only the flowers have spines | The spines dissolve in skin after a minute
test: A boy in Queensland wants to sit down on a sandy mound covered with small red ants. What should he do? | Find another spot away from the ant hill | Sit down very carefully and slowly, so the ants do not notice | Brush the ants off and then sit | Stamp on the mound to flatten it first
test: Which is true about stings? | A wasp can sting many times and does not die | A honeybee can sting again and again without harm | Bumblebees always die after one sting | All insects that sting die afterwards
test: A class on a nature trail is shouting and running. What will most likely happen? | The animals hide, so nobody sees them | More birds come to watch the class | The animals become calmer | Nothing, noise has no effect on animals
explain: A boy in California sat on a fire ant nest at a picnic; hundreds of ants stung him, and he had painful blisters for days.
explain: A dog off its lead in Wales chased a field of lambs; several were hurt, and the farmer was allowed by law to stop the dog.
fact: Fire ants sting many times each, and many stings at once can make a person ill.
fact: Dogs chasing sheep kill and injure many lambs every spring.
test: big ant hill at the park, can we sit here | No, move away; fire ants can sting many times | Yes, ants are harmless, they're far too small to hurt | Yes, if you sit still | Yes, put a blanket on it
test: lets let the dog off lead in the lamb field | Keep it on the lead; dogs chase and hurt lambs | Yes, it will play nicely | Yes, if it's small | Yes, if the farmer isn't there
test: stung by a bee, finger puffing a bit | Scrape the sting out, cool it and tell an adult | Squeeze it hard | Put mud on it | Ignore it and keep playing
test: did u hear the ground shook ages at the beach and some people "stayed to film the sea go out". lesson? | After a long or strong quake at the coast, go to high ground at once | Stay and watch the sea, coz it's amazing to film when it goes out | Quakes at the beach are harmless | Go into the water to check
test: my mate says "every bee dies when it stings, so they can only get u once". true? | No, wasps and bumblebees can sting again and do not die | Yes, every bee and wasp dies | Yes, it's a scientific fact | Yes, but only in summer
test: kids say the farmer shot a dog "just for barking" and are naming whose it was. spread it? | No, it's rumour; just keep dogs on a lead near sheep | Yes, name the owner | Yes, and say the farmer is evil | Yes, post it online
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the formation of a rainbow? | Sunlight bends inside raindrops and splits into colours | Clouds reflect the colours of the ground | Rain falls in coloured drops, each with its own colour | Light from the Moon divides in the air
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a person cannot reach the end of a rainbow. | It depends on where the observer stands and moves with them | It is located far out over the sea, beyond the horizon | It disappears at nightfall | It is guarded by law
exam: It is given that blackberries are to be picked. Select the response consistent with established guidance. | Only those identified by a grown-up, above dog height and washed | Any dark berries near the road, provided that they appear ripe | Berries from the lowest branches | Unwashed berries straight from the bush
exam: State the hazard posed by fire ant nests. | Fire ants sting many times, and many stings can make a person ill | Fire ants are harmless to people, being far too small to sting | Fire ants only bite plants | Fire ants sting once and then die
